,"text":"To solve the given problem, we start by analyzing the functional equation and the given initial value.\n\nGiven:\n\\[\n\\psi(1) = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}\n\\]\nand the functional equation:\n\\[\n\\psi(3x) + 3 \\psi(x) = \\psi^3(x).\n\\]\n\nWe need to determine \\(\\prod_{n=1}^{100} \\psi(3^n)\\).\n\nFirst, we will investigate the behavior of \\(\\psi(x)\\) by substituting specific values into the functional equation.\n\n1. **Substitute \\(x = 1\\):**\n\\[\n\\psi(3 \\cdot 1) + 3 \\psi(1) = \\psi^3(1).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\psi(3) + 3 \\psi(1) = \\psi^3(1).\n\\]\nGiven \\(\\psi(1) = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}\\), let us denote this value by \\(a\\):\n\\[\na = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}.\n\\]\nThen the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\psi(3) + 3a = a^3.\n\\]\n\n2. **Substitute \\(x = 3\\):**\n\\[\n\\psi(3 \\cdot 3) + 3 \\psi(3) = \\psi^3(3).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\psi(9) + 3 \\psi(3) = \\psi^3(3).\n\\]\n\n3. **Substitute \\(x = 9\\):**\n\\[\n\\psi(3 \\cdot 9) + 3 \\psi(9) = \\psi^3(9).\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\psi(27) + 3 \\psi(9) = \\psi^3(9).\n\\]\n\nWe observe a pattern where each \\(\\psi(3^n)\\) is related to \\(\\psi(3^{n-1})\\) through the functional equation. To find a general form, we hypothesize that \\(\\psi(3^n) = a\\).\n\nAssume \\(\\psi(3^n) = a\\) for all \\(n\\). Then we need to verify if this assumption holds for the functional equation:\n\\[\n\\psi(3 \\cdot 3^n) + 3 \\psi(3^n) = \\psi^3(3^n).\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(\\psi(3^n) = a\\), we get:\n\\[\na + 3a = a^3.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n4a = a^3.\n\\]\nSolving for \\(a\\), we get:\n\\[\na^3 - 4a = 0 \\implies a(a^2 - 4) = 0 \\implies a(a - 2)(a + 2) = 0.\n\\]\nThus, \\(a = 0\\), \\(a = 2\\), or \\(a = -2\\).\n\nGiven \\(\\psi(1) = \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}\\), we must choose the positive root, so \\(a = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, \\(\\psi(3^n) = 2\\) for all \\(n\\).\n\nFinally, we need to find:\n\\[\n\\prod_{n=1}^{100} \\psi(3^n).\n\\]\nSince \\(\\psi(3^n) = 2\\) for all \\(n\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\prod_{n=1}^{100} 2 = 2^{100}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2^{100}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2^{100}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
